## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Solvent effects on chemical pathways of perovskite formation. We first synthesized and isolated the intermediates INT-1, 2, 3, 6, and 7 (Fig. 3; all except INT-1 were obtained as µm-sized fibers), occurring under the conditions of the standard SC device fabrication as described in the Methods section (a \(30{-}50\ \mathrm{wt}\%\) PI and MAI solution in DMSO or DMF heated at \(60\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)). The compounds in red boxes were characterized by single crystal X-ray crystallographic analysis for the first time in this work (INT-2 and 3) or previously (INT-1, 6, and \(7^{21}\)), and those in blue boxes (INT-4 and 5) were identified by in situ X-ray diffraction (XRD) in this work (Fig. S1-S3). Fig. 3 summarizes their reactions as revealed by in situ 2-D XRD analysis of a sample held on a loop (cf. Fig. 3b). Scanning electron microscopic (SEM) images supplemented the XRD crystallographic information.
The reaction mediated by DMF (Fig. 3c) is mechanistically simpler than the DMSO-mediated reaction and described first. A mixture of PI and MAI in hot DMF directly produces a key intermediate, a di-nuclear plumbate (i.e., \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})_{3}\mathrm{~})^{22,23}\) isolated
as a crystalline fiber (INT-7, \((\mathrm{MA}^{+})_{2}(\mathrm{PbI}_{3}{}^{-})_{2}\mathrm{{\bulletDMF}}_{2}\), step xvi), which easily loses DMF to produce a PV fiber \((\mathrm{MA}^{+}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}{}^{-})\) under nitrogen at \(20{-}100\ \mathrm{~}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (step xviii). This PV fiber is brittle and fractured as seen by SEM (Fig. 3d). The formation
of INT-7 from PI was found to occur in two steps: First, exfoliation and truncation of a 2-D sheet of PI in hot DMF give reversibly a polymer strip of neutral \((\mathrm{PbI}_{2})_{2}\) (INT-6, crystalline fiber, steps xx and xxi). Second, supply of MAI by surface coating of INT-6 results in rapid ligand exchange at \(25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to give INT-7 (step xvii). The PV fiber is stable at 100 \(^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under nitrogen, but regenerates PI at \(>\!130\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) by loss of MAI (step xx). Notably, under DMF vapor, the PV fiber spontaneously regenerates INT-7 at \(25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) within \(0.5~\mathrm{h}\) (step xix); that is, the DMF molecules act as a base to extract acidic MAI molecules out of the \(\mathrm{PbI}_{3}{}^{-}\)crystal lattice, and \(\mathrm{PbI}_{3}{}^{-}\)is converted to the di-nuclear cluster INT-7. This process will be discussed later for Fig. 4, where \(\mathrm{MA}^{+}\mathrm{DMF}\) hydrogen bonding interaction is illustrated.
The DMSO reaction is similar to the DMF reaction except for a few important details (Fig. 3a). A mixture of PI and MAI in hot DMSO produces a new class of tri-nuclear plumbate as fibers (INT-3, \((\mathrm{MA}^{+})_{2}(\mathrm{PbI}_{3}^{-})_{2}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}]\cdot\mathrm{DMSO}_{2}\), step iii). Very smooth surface of the fibers is notable (Fig. 2g). The structure was determined by single crystal analysis (Fig. 4d, e). Dropcasting on ITO and slow evaporation of the same mixture of PI and MAI in DMSO also produces the INT-3 fibers (Fig. S4). An entity that shows the same XRD pattern as INT-3 was previously assigned to be \(\mathrm{MA}^{+}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}{}^{-}\cdot\mathrm{DMSO}\) based on elemental analysis.24 INT-3 also forms in two steps from PI: INT-1 and INT-2 are synthesized under reaction conditions of subtle difference (see the Methods section; steps i and ii as well as xii and xiii). When coated with MAI, mono-nuclear INT-1 gives tri-nuclear INT-3 within \(30~\mathrm{s}\) at \(25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (step iv), while di-nuclear INT-2 gives it more slowly (step v).
Because INT-3 \(((\mathrm{PbI}_{3}^{-})_{2}\mathrm{PbI}_{2})\) needs one additional MAI molecule to make PV \((3(\mathrm{PbI}_{3}^{-}))\), heating at \(100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) produces a 1:2 mixture of PI and PV (step vi). When INT-3 fibers are coated with MAI, intrusion of MAI into the fibers occurs to produce INT-4, followed by loss of two DMSO molecules to produce PV via INT-5 (steps vii–ix). INT-4 and INT-5 were identified by 2-D XRD analysis (vide infra). The PV fiber produced in this way from INT-3 is highly crystalline, as supported by SEM (Fig. 2h), XRD, selected-area electron diffraction of the fiber (SAED, Fig. S5). The INT-3-to-PV conversion accompanies mechanical stiffening of the fiber and a color change from yellow to black with a ratio of \(\mathrm{Pb:I}=1{:}3\) (Fig. S6 and Fig. S7). Like DMF, DMSO vapor quickly extract MAI to convert PV fiber into INT-3 fiber at \(25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (step x). The inter conversion between PV and INT-3 is so fast that exposure of the crystalline PV fibers to DMSO vapor at \(25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) causes quick degradation of the fiber to powder via INT-3, followed by Ostwald ripening of crystals, as seen in SEM images in Fig. 2g–j.
Single crystal structure analysis of Intermediates. Fig. 4a–e, j, k shows the crystal structures of \(\mathbf{P}\mathbf{I},^{25}\mathbf{INT}\mathbf{-1},^{19}\mathbf{2},\,3,\,6,^{20}\) and \(7^{21}\) as viewed approximately along the \((\mathrm{PbI}_{2})_{n}\) polymer chains (only two sets of \(\mathrm{Pb}\) atoms are shown for clarity). All of these structures have in common a \(\upmu\) -iodo-bridged, square bipyramidal \((\mathrm{PbI}_{2})_{n}\) polymer chain (Fig. 4a–e, j, k).26 The \((\mathrm{PbI}_{3}^{-})_{2}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) structure in INT-3 is stabilized by hydrogen-bonding network of \(\mathrm{MA}^{+}\mathrm{DMSO}\) (Fig. 4e). INT-7 is similarly arranged in the crystal (Fig. 4k).
The very facile conversion of PV back to INT-3 (step x) suggests their topological similarity, which we can identify by superimposing the crystal structure of INT-3 on the cubic lattice of \(\mathbf{PV}^{15}\) (Fig. 4 middle panel). Here, we see that the motion of each tri-nuclear plumbate in a pair along the red arrow directions converts INT-3 (Fig. 4g) to PV or vice versa (Fig. 4h, i; see Fig. 1 for the experimental crystal structure of PV on which Fig. 4h and 4i were drawn). In this way, the polymer strips of INT-3 laid along the fiber axis are zipped together to form a cubic PV crystal with minimum atomic motions (Fig. 2a–f). In the reverse process that occurs quickly at \(25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), DMSO extracts MA1 from the cubic \(\mathrm{PbI}_{3}^{-}\)lattice of
PV to form \(\mathrm{MA^{+}}\)•DMSO hydrogen-bonded to the tri-nuclear \((\mathrm{PbI}_{3}^{-})_{2}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) of INT-3. The same applies to the correlation between INT-7 and PV shown in Fig. 4l–n. These processes accompany rotation of the crystal \(a\) -axis lattice of INT-3 by ca. \(45^{\circ}\), which was experimentally verified by 2-D XRD analysis, as described next.
2-D XRD analysis of interconversion between INT-3 and PV. Experimental evidence for the \(45^{\circ}\) rotation was provided by 2-D XRD analysis of free-standing fibers (cf. Fig. 3b), which also allowed us to in situ study the PV crystal annealing process (Fig. 5a–d for DMSO and 5e–h for DMF). Fig. 5a shows a 2-D XRD image of several parallel fibers of the
plumbate INT-3, where (002), (021) and (022) diffractions are aligned linearly, indicating that the 1-D polymer axis (crystal \(a\)-axis) is parallel to the fiber axis. The PV fiber obtained by first heating INT-3 (DMSO) in Fig. 5b shows six spots on the (100) circle, two on the horizontal line and four with \(45^{\circ}\) inclination. Thus, as shown in the inset (Fig. 5b), the (110) normal runs parallel to the fiber axis (Fig. 4i), leaving two of the axes to make \(45^{\circ}\) and the other \(90^{\circ}\) angles to the fiber axis. Crystal annealing was examined next. Heating at \(100\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) converted INT-3 to PV (Fig. 5b). Sequential treatment of the
PV fibers with DMSO vapor at \(25\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and with heating at 100 \(^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) regenerated first partially oriented INT-3 (Fig. 5c) and then PV that shows random crystal orientation (Fig. 5d). Fig. 2i illustrates such randomness. Regenerated INT-3 shows an interesting feature; the INT-3 crystals have grown mainly in a direction orthogonal to the fiber axis (cf. Fig. 5c vs. 5a); that is, the crystals grew from the surface towards the center, as DMSO molecules diffused from the surface.
Similarly to DMSO, in Fig. 5e for INT-7 from DMF, we see that the 1-D polymer axis (crystal \(a\)-axis) is also parallel to the fiber axis. However, Fig 5f shows two additional spots at upper and bottom tangential positions, indicating that it is a ca. 1:1 mixture of crystals (inset) whose (110) normal runs both parallel and orthogonal to the fiber axis. As to the crystal annealing, heating/DMF-annealing of INT-7 rapidly randomize the crystal orientation (Fig. 5h), indicating that DMF is an effective solvent for production of polycrystalline PV powder.
We consider that the difference in the crystal volume change during conversion of INT-3 or INT-7 into PV affects the crystal quality of PV fibers. INT-3 takes one MAI and loses two DMSO molecules to produce PV (steps vii–ix), resulting in only \(13\%\) decrease of the crystal volume \((285\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3}\) to 247 \(\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3}/\mathrm{Pb}\) at \(20\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\). On the other hand, INT-7 loses two DMF molecules to produce PV (step xviii), resulting in a \(30\%\) decrease \((354\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3}\) to \(247\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3}/\mathrm{Pb}\) at \(20\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\).
Temperature-dependent in situ 1-D XRD analysis tells us the time-course of the DMSO-mediated reaction (Fig. 5i, j). As the ambient nitrogen temperature was increased from \(20\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to
\(130\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), INT-3 coated with MAI changes to PV in three stages via INT-4 \((2\theta=5.7^{\circ})\) and INT-5 \((2\theta=7.9^{\circ})\) (Fig. S8). The decrease of the smallest \(2\theta\) value of \(6.6^{\circ}\) in INT-3 to \(5.7^{\circ}\) in INT-4 (i.e., spacing increase of ca. \(0.21\ \mathrm{nm}\)) suggests an MAI uptake as the first step at \({>}30\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), followed by loss of one DMSO molecule to form INT-5 and another to form PV. In contrast, the di-nuclear plumbate INT-7 loses DMF molecules at \({>}50\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and produces PV in a single step (Fig. 5k and 5l).
Photovoltaic device and photodetectors. We describe finally that this oriented PV fiber made from INT-3 shows excellent optoelectronic properties as examined for photovoltaic (Fig. 6a, and Fig. S9). Made entirely free from solvent, this fiber exhibits a high chemical stability and ohmic contact with gold electrodes, and optoelectronic properties comparable to a single-block crystal. In contrast, the PV fiber annealed under DMSO vapor exposure hardly maintains its structural integrity for device applications (cf. Fig. 2i), indicating that quality control of the PV fiber is a worthwhile future task for realization of PV-based microdevices. Fig. 6b indicates that the photocurrent density of the fiber device (red) is comparable with that of a \(200\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\)-thick perovskite block-crystal device reported recently (blue).
We describe the photovoltaic devices in some detail. We transferred a single fiber of INT-3 onto the silicon wafer covered with a \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) layer \((470\ \mathrm{nm})\), heated them at \(130\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\ \mathrm{min}\) under nitrogen, and finally vacuum deposited Au (50 nm) and Ag \((100\ \mathrm{nm})\) on fibers separated from each other by \(25\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\). We stabilized the device by spin coating with CYTOP followed by heating at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\). The characteristics of these devices summarized under irradiation with different light intensities. The PV fiber device shows a power conversion efficiency (PCE) of \(1.35\%\) (average \(1.27\%\)) at a light power of \(8.1\ \mathrm{mW/cm}^{2}\) with a short-circuit current density \((J_{\mathrm{sc}})\) of \(0.567\ \mathrm{mA/cm}^{2}\), an open-circuit voltage \((V_{\mathrm{oc}})\) of \(0.65\ \mathrm{V}\) and a fill factor (FF) of \(29.7\%\). Since the fiber is thin (ca. \(500\ \mathrm{nm}\)), its contact with the electrodes is limited and the photocurrent gradually saturated under high-power light irradiance.
We also fabricated a photodetector on silicon wafer covered with a \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) layer \((300\ \mathrm{nm})\) by depositing two parallel \(100\ \mathrm{nm}\) thick Au electrodes over a single PV nanofiber followed by coating with a film CYTOP (details in experimental section). The observed linearity of the current–voltage curve at different light powers indicated ohmic contact between the nanofibers and gold electrodes (Fig. 7). The devise was found to show respectable responsivities of \(1.1\ \mathrm{A/W}\) at \(8.1\ \mathrm{mW/cm}^{2}\) and \(0.23\ \mathrm{A/W}\) at \(194.1\ \mathrm{mW/cm}^{2}\).
