## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Materials. \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) (99.999% purity) was purchased from Strem Chemicals, Inc. and used without further purification. MAI was prepared by using the procedure reported in Ref. 2.
Preparation of INT-1 (mono-nuclear strips of \((\mathbf{PbI}_{2})_{n}\), \(\mathbf{PbI}_{2}\mathbf{\bulletDMSO}_{2}\)). A \(30\,\mathrm{\wt\%}\) \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) solution was prepared by adding \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) \((448\,\mathrm{~mg})\) to DMSO/γ-butyrolactone (GBL) (1.0 mL, 3.7 vol/vol ratio) and stirred for \(12\,\mathrm{~h~}\) at \(60\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The solution was then cooled to room temperature to obtain crystals of INT-1.
Preparation of INT-2 (di-nuclear strips of \((\mathbf{PbI}_{2})_{n}\), \((\mathbf{PbI}_{2})_{2}\mathbf{\bulletDMSO}_{2}\)). A \(40\ \mathrm{wt\%}\) \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}/\mathrm{MAI}\) solution was prepared by adding \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) \((555~\mathrm{mg})\) and MAI \((192~\mathrm{mg})\) in DMSO/GBL \((1.0\ \mathrm{mL},\ 3{\cdot}7\ \mathrm{vol/vol}\) ratio) and stirred for \(12~\mathrm{{h}}\) at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The solution was cooled to room temperature, and \(10{-}20\ \mathrm{vol\%}\) of toluene added to obtain fibers of INT-2.
Preparation of INT-3 (tri-nuclear strips of \((\mathbf{PbI}_{2})_{n}\) coordinated by two iodide ions, \((\mathrm{MA}^{+})_{2}[(\mathrm{PbI}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}]\bullet\mathrm{DMSO}_{2}\)). A \(50\;\mathrm{wt\%}\) \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}/\mathrm{MAI}\) solution was prepared by adding \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\)
\((833~\mathrm{mg})\) and MAI \((287\;\mathrm{mg})\) in DMSO/GBL \((1.0\;\mathrm{mL},\,3{\cdot}7\) vol/vol ratio) and stirred for \(12~\mathrm{{h}}\) at \(60\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The solution was cooled to room temperature to obtain fibers of INT-3.
Preparation of INT-6 (di-nuclear strips of \((\mathbf{PbI}_{2})_{n}\), \((\mathbf{PbI}_{2})_{2}\mathbf{\bulletDMF}_{2}\)). A \(30\;\mathrm{wt\%}\) \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) solution was prepared by adding \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) \((405~\mathrm{mg})\) to DMF \((1.0\;\mathrm{mL})\) and stirred for \(12~\mathrm{{h}}\) at \(60\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The solution was then cooled to room temperature to obtain fibers of INT-6.
Preparation of INT-7 (di-nuclear strips of \((\mathbf{PbI}_{2})_{n}\) coordinated by two iodide ions, \((\mathbf{MA}^{+})_{2}(\mathbf{PbI}_{3})_{2}\mathbf{\bulletDMF}_{2}\)). A \(50\;\mathrm{wt\%}\) \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}/\mathrm{MAI}\) solution was prepared by adding \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) \((702~\mathrm{mg})\) and MAI \((242~\mathrm{mg})\) in DMF \((1.0\;\mathrm{mL})\) and stirred for \(12~\mathrm{{h}}\) at \(60\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The solution was cooled to room temperature to obtain fibers of INT-7.
Crystallographic analysis of single crystals INT-1–3. The diffraction images for X-ray crystallographic analysis were collected on a Rigaku Rapid II diffractometer equipped with an imaging plate (IP) using \(\mathrm{Cu}\;\mathrm{K}_{\alpha}\) \((\lambda=1.5419\mathrm{~\AA~})\) radiation. For crystallographic analysis, the sample was removed from the mother liquor and put onto a glass slide. A crystal was chosen and then it was put on and taken off from a glass slide several times to remove liquid from its surface.
Fiber XRD diffraction analyses. The diffraction images for fiber X-ray analyses were collected on a Rigaku Rapid II diffractometer equipped with an IP using \(\mathrm{Cu}\;\mathrm{K}_{\alpha}\) \((\lambda=1.5419\mathrm{~\AA~})\) radiation. Diffraction images were taken under the following conditions. Exposure time: \(1\ \mathrm{\s/^{\circ}}\), \(\mathrm{d}\omega\): \(180^{\circ}\). The fiber was placed with its fiber axis perpendicular to the horizontal line.
The fiber was kept under nitrogen atmosphere during measurement. A heater attached to the nitrogen generator controlled the temperature. Image acquisition took \(5\,\textrm{m i n}\) (3 min for exposure, \(2\;\mathrm{min}\) for IP reading).
For the fibers coated with MAI and DMSO, first, a bundle of fibers was removed from the mother liquor by tweezers and put on a glass slide. Then a sample loop was used to pick up multiple fibers without removing liquid from their surface and mounted on a goniometer.
For fibers without MAI and DMSO, first, a sample loop was used to pick up a single fiber. To remove liquid from the surface, the fiber was dipped in a droplet of dichloromethane and then mounted on a goniometer.
The conversion from INT-3 to PV was performed in the following manner. INT-2 fibers were put on a glass slide without removing solvent surrounding them. The sample was left under ambient conditions until most of the solvent evaporated (for \(2\;\mathrm{h}\)) to obtain INT-3 fibers covered with MAI. A bundle of fibers aligned in the same direction was then taken by a sample loop and mounted on a goniometer. INT-3 fibers were heated from \(20~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to \(100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with an interval of 5 min for each \(10~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Conversion to PV was completed around \(0.5\mathrm{~h~}\) after the temperature reached \(100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The conversion quickly finished at \(130\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
The conversion from INT-7 to PV was studied in a similar manner as for INT-3–PV conversion. INT-7 fibers were prepared directly from solution. A single INT-7 fiber was heated from \(20~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with an interval of \(5\;\mathrm{min}\) for each \(10\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The conversion completed when the temperature reached \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
For the vapor-treatment experiment, the PV fiber sample was prepared from INT-3 or INT-7 as described above. The PV fiber sample was kept for \(0.5\;\mathrm{h}\) under nitrogen saturated with DMSO or DMF vapor. The fiber XRD measurement was performed at \(20\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Conversion to PV finished at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
Preparation of photovoltaic device and photodetector. A lateral photovoltaic device or photodetector using PV fiber was fabricated on a silicon wafer covered with a \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) layer \((470\;\mathrm{nm})\). Two parallel asymmetric (Au and Ag) or symmetric (Au only) metal electrodes were vacuum deposited on a single PV fiber made from an INT-3 fiber (without washing) heating at \(130\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/10\ \mathrm{min}\). A MAX-303 xenon lamp (VIS) light source from ASAHI SPECTRA illuminated the photovoltaic device and photo detector from the top of the device. The value of photocurrent density in Figure 6 refers to the short circuit current density of the device.
The photodetector showed respectable photoresponse characteristics, working at a voltage below \(2\,\mathrm{~V~}\) with dark current of \(0.04\ \mathrm{\nA}\). Responsivity (R) was calculated by following equation:
\[
\mathrm{R}=(\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{light}}-\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{dark}})/\mathrm{P}_{\mathrm{light}}
\]
